.DS_Store
.pio
.vscode/.browse.c_cpp.db*
.vscode/c_cpp_properties.json
.vscode/launch.json
.vscode/ipch
data/
firmware/src_appliance/fw_version.h
firmware/src_config/fw_version.h
firmware/src_display/fw_version.h
firmware/src_faucet/fw_version.h
firmware/src_front/fw_version.h
firmware/src_pcba_bench/fw_version.h
firmware/src_prototype/fw_version.h
firmware/src_appliance/fw_build_time.h
firmware/src_prototype/fw_build_time.h
**/xcuserdata/
**/build/
__pycache__/
*.xcodeproj/
logs/*.log
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/
node_modules

# CAD script output (generated STEP/STL files)

# board-3d.py: the component-model download cache, manufacturer STEPs keyed by LCSC.
hardware/pcb/pcba/.cad-cache/

# A board's whole rendered set — the fab pack, the copper and mask views, the 3D assembly —
# written by hardware/pcb/pcba/render-board.ts on every save of a board and served off
# `pcb/<dir>/out/` by web/lib/walk.js. They arrive the road every solid takes: `pack.py`'s
# BUNDLED_BOARD_DIRS puts them in the release asset, hardware/cad-artifacts.lock.json names each
# by sha256, and web/scripts/fetch-cad-artifacts.mjs puts them on the disk a deploy serves from.
# The on-demand full B-rep and the routed circuit-json are held out of the bundle too; the
# board's own .gitignore carries the second.
hardware/pcb/pcba/out/

# Atomic-export temp files: hardware/_cadq_export.py writes a
# `.NAME.EXT.RANDOM.EXT` sibling then renames it atomically onto the target.
# A hard-killed run (SIGKILL/SIGTERM mid-write) can orphan one — never commit it.
.*.step.*.step
.*.stl.*.stl
.*.dxf.*.dxf
.*.pdf.*.pdf

# Remotion's lock file — installed by hand on one machine, and nothing else reads it.
# `tools/render`'s is NOT here: the derive workflow installs those renderers too, and what
# they draw is committed. An unpinned puppeteer renders the same card to different pixels,
# which is a diff on every run in files the tree holds.
marketing/video/remotion/package-lock.json

# Remotion channel foundation: render outputs and caches are regenerable
marketing/video/remotion/out/
marketing/video/remotion/.remotion/
.firebase-setup/
tools/flavor_preview.png

# Local CadQuery venv (tools/cad-venv/bin/python) — regenerable, machine-specific.
# No trailing slash: a checkout that links the toolchain in rather than installing it
# has a symlink here, and a trailing slash matches only a directory.
tools/cad-venv

# Tessellation written beside each STEP for the viewer to read instead of parsing it
# (hardware/scripts/_cadq_export.py). `loadStepFile` prefers it and parses the STEP where there
# is none — and on the enclosure the payload is where the show surface is, the solid beside it a
# smooth prism (hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/flute_skin.py), so that parse draws a
# box with no flutes on it. A deploy is served the payloads the bundle carries
# (tools/cad-artifacts/pack.py); hardware/scripts/check_payloads.py names one absent or older
# than the solid it stands for.
*.step.mesh

# The solids arrive in a release asset. hardware/cad-artifacts.lock.json names that asset and
# every solid in it by sha256, tools/cad-artifacts/pack.py builds and pins one, and
# web/scripts/fetch-cad-artifacts.mjs puts them on the disk a deploy serves from. The lock is
# what git carries.
*.step
# The placement pass hands this description directly from `//:enclosure-box` to the enclosure,
# ceiling and full-assembly actions. Direct design runs derive the live placement and never read
# a checkout copy, so retaining one here would only preserve a stale action intermediate.
hardware/manifold-layout/enclosure-box.json
# Except the harvested solids, which have no builder here — y_divider.py:3 says it of its own,
# and manifold_layout.py places the elbow and the tee. `pack.py`'s HARVESTED names the same three.
!hardware/reference/elbow-connector/elbow-connector.step
!hardware/reference/tee-connector/tee-connector.step
!hardware/reference/y-divider/y-divider.step

# Solids drawn once and kept (hardware/scripts/_realized.py). A miss costs what the build
# always cost; deleting this is always safe.
.cache/

# The seconds each generator's last runs took, on the machine that ran them
# (hardware/scripts/_build_time.py). What ledger/build-time.md carries is the figure these
# settle on; a checkout that has run nothing has no file here and moves no figure.
hardware/ledger/.build-time.samples.json
hardware/ledger/.build-time.samples.json.tmp

# Scene STEPs are a rendering intermediate for the unit cards' pictures — a cut
# of enclosure-assembly.step, regenerated on demand by
# hardware/assembly/scenes/render_scenes.py. What is committed is the PNG in
# cards/img/ and the fingerprint beside it; the STEPs would churn on every move
# of any body they contain, which is exactly the commit cost the pictures must
# not add.
hardware/assembly/scenes/out/
bazel-*

# Where this checkout is, which is not where any other checkout is. `gen_build.py` writes it
# and `.bazelrc` takes it with `try-import`, so BUILD.bazel carries no machine's path.
.bazelrc.paths
# The flags a CI lane adds, written by the workflow so every bazel invocation in the job
# reads them rather than only the command line that names one.
.bazelrc.ci

# A card's own two renders — the PNG a printer is handed one card at a time, and the page it
# contributes to the bound deck — drawn from the card HTML by a browser
# (hardware/assembly/cards/_build.py). `//:cards-build` names them in `outs` and none in `srcs`,
# no web route serves one and no doc embeds one. The build makes them, sync_tree lands them on
# the disk, and nothing carries them into a commit. THE BOUND DECK IS NOT HERE: `deck.pdf` sits
# beside the cards, in git, because the site serves it and a deploy fetches nothing.
hardware/assembly/cards/out/

# A quick-start sheet's own two renders, the same pair for the same reasons
# (hardware/quickstart/_build.py).
hardware/quickstart/out/

# The bound Quick Start and the pictures in it, drawn by hardware/quickstart/quickstart_art.py
# and _build.py off the same solids as everything else. They arrive the road every solid takes:
# `pack.py`'s BUNDLED_ART_DIRS and BUNDLED_ART_FILES put them in the release asset,
# hardware/cad-artifacts.lock.json names each by sha256, and web/scripts/fetch-cad-artifacts.mjs
# puts them on the disk a deploy serves from — `walkDocuments` finds the PDF there through its
# sidecar. The `style.css` in the same directories and everything under `studies/` are written
# by hand and are in git.
hardware/quickstart/art/*.png
hardware/quickstart/art/colors.css
hardware/quickstart/quick-start.cover.png
hardware/quickstart/quick-start.pdf
hardware/quickstart/quick-start.pdf.json

# The scene meshes the parts viewer opens as themselves, cut by
# hardware/assembly/scenes/render_scenes.py. `web/public/js/viewer/parts.js` names `glb` a build
# directory and hands one to `openGlbDetail`. They arrive the road every solid takes:
# `pack.py`'s BUNDLED_GLB_DIRS puts them in the release asset, hardware/cad-artifacts.lock.json
# names each by sha256, and web/scripts/fetch-cad-artifacts.mjs puts them on the disk a deploy
# serves from. The lock is what git carries.
hardware/assembly/scenes/glb/

# Fetched at deploy from the release firmware/firmware.lock.json names
# (web/scripts/fetch-firmware.mjs). The lock is committed; the images are not.
web/public/firmware/
